<!--
    An inline system call (`syscall`) lifts to a CALLOTHER with no register inputs
    and no output, so the v0 a function sets up for it still holds that value
    afterwards. `number` returns 0xfa4, the syscall number it loaded into v0, and `after`
    adds 0xfb4 to its argument.

    Stage / sub-stage: P2 flow-classification.
    P0 assertion:      option syscallregs off|auto|on  (default auto).

    Source (clang -O2 -target mipsel-linux-gnu -c, the .text bytes verbatim below):

        int number(int a, int b) {
          register int v0 asm("$2") = 4004;
          register int a0 asm("$4") = b; register int a1 asm("$5") = a;
          asm volatile("syscall" : "+r"(v0) : "r"(a0), "r"(a1) : "memory", "$7");
          return v0;
        }
        int after(int x) {
          register int v0 asm("$2") = 4020;
          asm volatile("syscall" : "+r"(v0) :: "memory", "$7");
          if (v0 < 0) return -1;
          return v0 + x;
        }
        int fence(int a) { asm volatile("sync" ::: "memory"); return a; }

    Pass 1 (option syscallregs on) gives the syscall its effects: it writes v0,
    and it reads the number register v0 and then the argument registers in
    order, up to the first one the function did not write on every path to
    it, and at least as many as the kernel's entry point for the number takes:
    write (4004) takes three, so number also hands the kernel the a2 its
    caller left, which becomes its third parameter.  Pass 2 (option syscallregs off) is the old model, and so is pass 3,
    the default `auto`: it acts only on an image the loader identified as a
    program for an operating system's user space, which a bare byte chunk is
    not.  fence is the control: its barrier is a CALLOTHER too,
    and nothing about it changes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
250880002520a00025282000a40f02240c0000000800e00300000000a40f0224
0c000000230802000a0847000800e00325102000b40f02240c00000021084400
00004328ffff02240800e0030a1023000f0000000800e00325108000
